---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Configuration"
  description="Understand the .considered.yaml configuration file."
  current="/configuration/"
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1>Configuration</h1>
    <p>
      The configuration file is <code>.considered.yaml</code> at the repository
      root. It defines the standards to evaluate, the files to exclude, and any
      reviewed variances. It can also include guidance for coding agents.
    </p>

    <p>
      This example enables all exclusion categories. The starter written by
      <code>considered init</code> enables only <code>tests</code>,
      <code>vendored</code>, and <code>dependencies</code>, plus gitignored files.
    </p>

    <pre><code>instructions: |
  Prefer small, focused changes.
  Preserve public APIs unless a variance explains the change.

kinds:
  - performance
  - compatibility

exclude:
  gitignored: true
  categories:
    - assets
    - configuration
    - documentation
    - generated
    - tests
    - vendored
    - dependencies
  paths:
    - src/generated/**

standards:
  scc.code_lines:
    max: 500
  scc.complexity:
    max: 50
  filesystem.bytes:
    max: 50000
  filesystem.longest_line:
    max: 140

warnings:
  percentBelowMax: 10
  percentAboveMin: 10</code></pre>

    <h2>Instructions</h2>
    <p>
      <code>instructions</code> is optional free-form text, primarily intended
      for coding agents. A text <code>considered check</code> prints it before
      violations. Run <code>considered instructions</code> to print only this
      content.
    </p>

    <h2>Kinds</h2>
    <p>
      <code>kinds</code> adds repository-specific variance kinds. Built-ins are
      <code>architectural</code>, <code>debt</code>, and <code>generated</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Standards</h2>
    <p>
      Standards are metric boundaries keyed by namespaced metric name. Each
      standard takes <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, or both.
    </p>

    <h2>Warnings</h2>
    <p>
      Warnings report passing values that are close to a boundary.
      <code>percentBelowMax: 10</code> warns when a passing value is at least
      90% of a max boundary. <code>percentAboveMin: 10</code> warns when a
      passing value is at most 110% of a min boundary. Percentages must be
      between 0 and 100. Omit a threshold to disable that warning direction;
      percentage warnings apply only to positive boundaries.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Warnings never fail the check. They apply to both standards and approved
        variance boundaries.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
